[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Animations in Android: Property, View, and Transition APIs"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Animations breathe life into Android applications, providing visual feedback and guiding user attention. Android offers three primary animation frameworks: View Animations, Property Animations, and the Transition Framework. This tutorial explores each approach using Java."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. View Animations (Tween Animations)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"View animations are the oldest form of animations in Android. They are easy to implement via XML and Java, but they only animate the visual representation of a View, not the View itself (meaning click bounds do not change)."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"Animation fadeIn = AnimationUtils.loadAnimation(context, android.R.anim.fade_in);
fadeIn.setDuration(1000);
myView.startAnimation(fadeIn);"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Property Animations (Introduced in API 11)"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Property Animation system is robust and flexible. It lets you animate any property of any object (whether it draws to the screen or not), including color, position, and rotation. The ObjectAnimator class is the most common tool for this."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"ObjectAnimator animator = ObjectAnimator.ofFloat(myButton, "translationX", 0f, 200f);
animator.setDuration(500);
animator.setInterpolator(new AccelerateDecelerateInterpolator());
animator.start();"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To run multiple property animations simultaneously or sequentially, use AnimatorSet."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"ObjectAnimator scaleX = ObjectAnimator.ofFloat(myView, "scaleX", 1f, 1.5f);
ObjectAnimator scaleY = ObjectAnimator.ofFloat(myView, "scaleY", 1f, 1.5f);

AnimatorSet set = new AnimatorSet();
set.playTogether(scaleX, scaleY);
set.setDuration(300);
set.start();"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Transition API"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Transition framework simplifies animating UI changes when switching between different layouts or scenes. It handles layout changes smoothly using AutoTransition or custom transitions."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"ViewGroup rootContainer = findViewById(R.id.root_container);

// Begin tracking changes for the transition
TransitionManager.beginDelayedTransition(rootContainer);

// Modify layout parameters
ViewGroup.Params params = myButton.getLayoutParams();
params.width = 500;
myButton.setLayoutParams(params);"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use View Animations for simple fading or scaling effects where touch bounds do not matter."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Property Animations for dynamic, interactive, and complex UI motion graphics."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the Transition API for scene changes and layout state modifications."}]
